      },
      "worked": "Step 1 is valid because $1-\\sin^2\\theta=\\cos^2\\theta$. Step 2 divides by $\\cos\\theta$, which may be zero. Factor instead:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta-\\cos^2\\theta&=0\\\\\\cos\\theta(\\sin\\theta-\\cos\\theta)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nThus $\\cos\\theta=0$ or $\\sin\\theta=\\cos\\theta$.\n\nFor $\\cos\\theta=0$, the unit circle gives $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$. Both satisfy the original equation because both sides are zero.\n\nFor $\\sin\\theta=\\cos\\theta$, $\\cos\\theta\\ne0$, so $\\tan\\theta=1$ and $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{4},\\frac{5\\pi}{4}$.\n\nThe complete solution set is $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{4},\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{5\\pi}{4},\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$. Step 2 loses $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
